<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tōhoku region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tohoku region, Tōhoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tōhoku region is the north-eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, the main island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, made up of the prefectures of Aomori, Iwate, Miyagi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita">Akita</a>, Yamagata and Fukushima. Largely the old provinces of Mutsu and Dewa, it was long a frontier of the Japanese state and later the base of clans such as the Date and Uesugi. Its Pacific coast was devastated by the earthquake and tsunami of March 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tohoku-region.jpg" alt="Tōhoku region" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tohoku_Region_in_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians generally link the rise of Ainu culture to the Satsumon culture, which followed the Epi-Jōmon in <b>Tōhoku</b> and Hokkaido, with later contributions from the Okhotsk culture of the north.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ainu">Ainu · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In Hokkaido and northern <b>Tōhoku</b> the Jōmon were followed instead by the related Zoku-Jōmon, or Epi-Jōmon, culture and by the Okhotsk culture, which later merged with or were replaced by the Satsumon culture around the 7th century CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The mix varied from place to place: Kyushu, Okinawa and the <b>Tōhoku region</b> kept stronger Jōmon traits, while Kansai and Shikoku show more pronounced Yayoi features.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Tōhoku</b> earthquake and tsunami of 11 March 2011, the Suntory company used it in commercials in which 71 actors and singers sang parts of it to encourage people to keep going, and it was heard the same year in the Studio Ghibli film From Up on…</blockquote><a href="/songs/sukiyaki">Sukiyaki (Ue o Muite Arukō)</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem celebrates the imperial edict on the discovery of gold in Michinoku Province (present-day <b>Tōhoku</b>) in 749.</blockquote><a href="/songs/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a></li><li><blockquote>The Date built power through marriage alliances with neighbouring families across the <b>Tohoku</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/date-clan-domain">Date clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
